Durarara!! AU
Chapter 44 – Part 2
Curtis shoved the couch back with one hand.
Tile. Space. Nothing for his head to catch but floor.
“Oh man. This is going to be wild.”
He sat. Breathed. Tried to give her the thing he was not allowed to give.
“There’s a mark. It—”
The sentence did not finish.
Blood shot from his mouth hard enough to hit the tile in a dark fan. His eyes went red at the corners and spilled. His nose followed, fast, like the fee had been waiting behind his teeth. Curtis slammed forward onto his hands, then his shoulder, a wet sound punched out of him that was not a word.
Mika clapped both hands over her own mouth.
She did not scream loud enough to make a neighbor a witness. She did not grab her phone. She half-rose off the chair and froze there, shaking, because that was the deal.
Curtis coughed and more red hit the floor. His braids dragged through it. The gauze at his hairline went useless. He tried to lift his head and the room tilted him back down.
“Don’t—” The rest broke into another mouthful. “—call.”
Mika’s eyes were huge.
“I won’t. I won’t. Oh my god. Curtis—”
He got one knee under himself and failed it. Forehead toward the tile. Fingers smearing a print beside the unused second knife from lunch.
The oni on the table watched with dead lenses.
The name of the mark never made it out.
The blowback did.
Curtis Nightstar bled on his own kitchen floor so a girl who had asked why would understand that can’t had never been a joke, and Mika Harima held the chair like it was the only promise she could still keep.
Curtis went down all the way.
The tension left his arms. His cheek hit the tile in his own blood. The coughing stopped. The awful wet sound stopped. What was left was a tall boy on a kitchen floor, braids in the red, gauze ruined, chest still moving if Mika stared hard enough to make it true.
She made a noise that wanted to be a scream and crushed it.
“Curtis. Curtis—”
No answer.
She dropped off the chair onto her knees just outside the fan of blood, hands hovering, not shaking him, not rolling him, because she did not know which part of him was allowed to be touched. The phone in her pocket felt like a live wire. Namie. Seiji. An ambulance. The deal.
You can’t freak out and you can’t call an ambulance.
Mika’s fingers shook over the screen and then locked.
“I won’t,” she whispered at him, furious and terrified. “You said it passes. So pass. Don’t you dare make me a liar.”
The apartment was very quiet.
The oni stared at the ceiling.
Lunch sat in the sink like a different afternoon.
Mika Harima sat on her heels in a stranger’s kitchen with a boy passed out in his blood and kept the only promise he had asked of her, counting his breaths because counting was the one thing she could do that was not a phone call.
Time went ugly and slow.
Then color came back into his skin like someone had turned a tap the other way. The grey left his mouth. His fingers twitched in the drying red.
Curtis blinked awake at the tile.
“…It still fuckin’ sucks.” A raw swallow. He didn’t try to sit yet. “But I hope you know I’m not trying to lie.”
Mika made a broken sound that was half a sob and half a swear.
“You passed out in a puddle. I counted your breathing like a psycho. Don’t talk to me about hope.”
“Didn’t call.”
“I wanted to.” Her hands were still fists in her sleeves. “I still want to. You look like a crime scene.”
Curtis pushed up onto an elbow, slow, grimacing at the taste. The gauze was a lost cause. Blood had painted one braid darker.
“That’s the fee. I start the name, it takes the sentence out of me.” He glanced at her, mismatched eyes clearer than they had any right to be. “Rose. Scythe. Smoke. Same lock. I’m not keeping a toy from you. I’m keeping you off this floor.”
Mika’s laugh came out wet.
“Mission failed. I’m on the floor.”
“You’re on the clean part. That’s something.” He sat the rest of the way, back to the cabinet, and let his head rest there. “Water. Then I clean. Then you can yell. Just—not an ambulance. Not Namie. This stays here.”
She grabbed a dish towel, soaked it, and shoved it at his hands without asking if she was allowed to help.
“Next time you want to prove you’re not a liar,” Mika said, voice shaking, “use words that don’t bleed. I’m still mad. I’m still here. Both.”
Curtis took the towel.
“Both is fair.”
The mark had taken its price and given nothing back but a girl who now believed him.
He closed his eyes once, just to make sure the room would stay, and started wiping his own mouth like lunch had never happened.
Curtis looked at the tile like it was an old argument.
“You’re like the sixth person who hasn’t believed me on the blowback. Justice did it as a messed up party trick.”
Mika’s face went tight.
“Isn’t that dangerous?”
“It could be. But both of our bodies are not normal anymore.” He glanced around at the spread, the soaked towel, the fan that had already started to dull. “This is a lot of Rh-null blood. One could make a decent amount off this.”
Mika stared at him.
“You have Rh-null?!”
“Yeah.” He said it the way he said the rest of the terrible answers — flat, already spent. “Rare. Annoying. Hospitals get excited. My parents found out the expensive way and decided I was a bill. That’s a different story.”
“You just bled a fortune onto your kitchen floor to prove a point.”
“I bled a fee onto my kitchen floor so you’d stop thinking I was being cute about a scythe.” Curtis wrung the towel out in the sink, water going pink. “Justice used to pop the sentence at parties when people called him a liar. Crowd loved it until they didn’t. I always thought it was stupid. Now I did it for coffee.”
Mika hugged her knees on the clean tile.
“Your body isn’t normal. His wasn’t. You have blood people would steal. You ran down a building with a white knife. And you cook like a gentleman.” She shook her head. “I don’t know which part I’m supposed to put first.”
“Put the food first. The rest is just why I don’t do magic tricks on command.” He caught her eyes. “Don’t tell Namie about the blood type. Don’t tell Seiji. That’s the kind of sentence that grows labs.”
Mika nodded, small and sharp.
“Muted,” she said. “Still muted. I’m not selling you to a building that already had me on a table.”
Curtis almost smiled.
“Good. Then we’re even on secrets we shouldn’t have seen.”
The Rh-null sat in the grout like another thing Ikebukuro was not allowed to inventory.
Mika did not ask to see the trick again.
Curtis sat against the cabinet a moment longer, then nodded at the mess like it had overstayed.
“Well. Since you already know some of my powers and know I have them. Watch this.”
He clasped his hands together.
White smoke tore out of him in thin, living lines — not the black Celty wore, not a stage fog. Tendrils. They hit the tile first and drank the red. They climbed the cabinet faces, the grout, the edge of the couch he had shoved back. The soaked towel lifted, wrung itself over the sink, and dropped neat. The fan of blood went from crime scene to clean kitchen in a handful of breaths.
Mika stopped hugging her knees.
The smoke did not touch her. It worked around her shoes like it had manners too.
“That’s—” She swallowed. “That’s what cleaned before I knocked.”
“Yeah.” The tendrils finished the floor, flicked over the table without knocking the oni, and pulled back into his palms as if they had never been loose. Curtis opened his hands. Empty. “Rose. Scythe. This. Same family. No name attached. No fee if I don’t try to explain the lock.”
The apartment smelled like soap and heat instead of iron.
Mika stared at the place where the blood had been.
“You could have done that first and skipped the dying.”
“You wouldn’t have believed the dying was real.” He stood, slower than the smoke, and pushed the couch back with ordinary hands. “Now you’ve seen both. The ugly one and the useful one. I’m still not giving you a manual.”
She let out a breath that shook.
“Okay. Fine. You’re a gentleman who bleeds rare blood and mops with ghosts.” A weak, crooked smile. “If that stuff ever grabs me I’m screaming. Just so you know.”
“It won’t. It knows the difference.”
Mika looked at him like the rider, the cook, and the boy on the tile had finally agreed to be one person.
“Still three,” she said, because she needed something human in the room.
Curtis huffed.
“Still not a door fee. Juice is in the fridge. Mask stays off.”
The smoke was gone.
The kitchen was clean.
She believed him now, and that was the part he could not put back in a box.
The last of the smoke vanished into his palms.
Mika watched his hands like they might open again.
“Who’s stronger. You or Justice.”
Curtis went quiet in a different way than the blowback.
“Hmm. It would be a tie.” He leaned on the counter, eyes on nothing in the room. “Same height on a bad day. Same stupid ideas. He made a room louder. I made it last. If we fought for real we’d have wrecked the forest and then sat down to eat like nothing happened.”
Mika waited.
Curtis’s mouth tightened.
“But after what happened… I’m starting to think more and more about where his mental health was at when he died.”
The oni on the table suddenly looked like a leftover from a party trick that had stopped being funny.
“He did the blowback for laughs. He promised he wouldn’t leave. Then he left anyway.” Curtis said it without decoration. “I used to measure us in who could run farther. Who could take a hit. Now I measure the days he was already gone behind the grin. I missed it. That’s on me.”
Mika’s voice came smaller.
“You think he was sick.”
“I think he was tired in a way a tie doesn’t fix.” He glanced at her, not asking her to carry the whole thing. “Strength was never the question. The question was whether I was looking at the right fight.”
Mika hugged her elbows.
“That’s a terrible thing to find out after kitchen magic.”
“Yeah.” A faint, honest breath. “You asked who’s stronger. Dead heat. Then he won by leaving first. I don’t like that score.”
She didn’t reach for three.
She didn’t reach for Seiji.
She sat in a clean kitchen with a boy who could mop blood off tile and still lose a tie he would have given anything to keep even.
Curtis stared at the clean tile like it had a calendar in it.
“To think it’s been a little over a month now.”
Mika went still.
“What?”
He didn’t dress it up.
“Justice. A little over a month.” The words sat in the kitchen with the leftover steam from lunch. “I packed. I flew. I started school. I put a mask on. It still feels like I could turn around and he’d be stealing food off the board.”
Mika’s mouth had gone thin.
“A month. You came here a month after your brother died and you’ve been… doing all of this.”
“List said college. List said Japan. I finished the flight. I didn’t finish him.” Curtis’s voice stayed level, which made it worse. “Ikebukuro thinks I’m a first-year with a bandage. I’m a first-year with a month.”
She sat down harder than the chair needed.
“That’s why the kisses and the hanging out and the I still want to see you sounded like a person running.” Her eyes shone and she hated it. “You’re not over it. You cooked me lunch in the middle of it.”
“I’m not using you to outrun it.” He met her stare. “I’m telling you the number because you asked who was stronger and the honest answer has a date on it.”
Mika dragged both hands down her face.
“Seiji picked a room. You picked a country with a hole in it.” A shaky breath. “I’m still here. I’m just— a month is nothing. That’s not even a season.”
“I know.”
The oni kept its dead eyes on the ceiling.
A little over a month sat between them, small and enormous, and Mika Harima, who had been reconstructed and hunted and kissed twice, finally understood that the gentleman with smoke in his hands was still standing in the first weeks after the tie had broken.
Curtis walked up to her.
He didn’t loom. He just closed the space the month had opened.
“You’re not a replacement. Or a coping mechanism.” His voice stayed quiet on purpose. “I told you. Because I trust you, Mika.”
She looked up at him like the name had weight.
“You just said it’s been a month.”
“That’s why I’m saying this now. Not because I need a stand-in for a dead brother or a hole in a list.” He held her eyes. Green. Blue. No lenses. “You asked questions I didn’t have to answer. You sat on the floor and didn’t call anyone. You muted a building to come eat. That’s trust going both ways. I’m not putting Justice on you. I’m not putting Seiji’s chair on you either. You’re Mika. That’s enough to tell.”
Her throat worked.
“People don’t say I trust you after they bleed on the tile.”
“I do.” A small, tired almost-smile. “I don’t have a lot of rooms I say it in. This one’s yours if you want it. Hang out. Food. No mask. No job. You’re not filling a gap. You’re here.”
Mika’s hands found the front of his shirt and stayed there, not climbing, not collecting.
“Okay,” she whispered. “Okay. I’m not a replacement. You’re not a rider in this kitchen. We can sit with the month and still have juice.”
“Yeah.”
He didn’t kiss her.
He let her hold the shirt.
Trust sat in the clean apartment with a month-old grief and two uncounted kisses, and for once Curtis Nightstar did not try to make the sentence smaller than it was.
